<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip IV of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1268">1268</a>–<a href="/years/1314">1314</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Philip the Fair</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, called the Fair, was king from 1285 to 1314. His marriage to Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> brought Champagne to the crown, and he seized La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and forced the Count of Bar to become his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 1301. He clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>, moved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> to take their wealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip IV of France" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_IV_of_France_1285-1314.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Philip IV of France</b> occupied the county in 1300, the townsmen of Bruges rose in the Bruges Matins of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the goedendag, destroyed the French cavalry…</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter and his successors strengthened ducal administration, and <b>Philip IV</b> made John II a peer of France in 1297.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip IV of France</b> annexed Lyon, formally an imperial city, in 1312, and the popes had settled at Avignon from 1309.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip IV the Fair</b> (r. 1285–1314) and his lawyers held that the king was emperor in his own kingdom, defied Pope Boniface VIII and summoned the first Estates-General in 1302.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1234 the crown passed to the counts of Champagne, then in 1274 to Joan I, whose marriage to <b>Philip IV</b> joined Navarre to France until 1328, and later to the houses of Évreux, Foix and Albret.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II of France expelled them from his lands in 1182 and recalled them in 1198; Edward I of England expelled all Jews from his kingdom in 1290, and <b>Philip IV of France</b> did the same in 1306.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li></ul></section>
